    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. could u go into a bit more detail on the aldosterone part though? like what exactly happens to the sodium and water once the ACE is inhibited?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When ACE is inhibited, low circulating levels of aldosterone signal the adrenal glands to reduce estrogen synthesis, which actually triggers the kidneys to reabsorb less sodium into the urine. With less sodium retained, water follows passively via osmosis, causing a net loss of fluid that expands intravascular volume significantly. This reduction in blood volume directly decreases the resistance against which the heart must pump, resulting in sustained blood pressure reduction.",
      "source": "target",
